ider: maybe the game over frame is a \"new level 1\" with a slightly different layout? The cups are in the same place, the magenta is in the same place, the charcoal is in the same place. The blue is at (16,12,19,31) instead of (16,8,19,27). The timer is full.\nIf this is a new run, I should act immediately with the fast plan:\n1. Move the blue to rows 12-15, columns 20-39: from (16,12,19,31): UP\u00d71 \u2192 rows 12-15; RIGHT\u00d72 \u2192 columns 20-39.\n2. SPACE.\n3. Observe.\nTotal: 4 actions. If nothing happens, next: DOWN\u00d78 \u2192 rows 44-47 (columns 20-39 already covers the left cup opening 20-23), SPACE. Observe.\nLet me execute this in a single batch, but insert checks between sub-batches. First batch: UP, RIGHT, RIGHT, SPACE. Then inspect.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "ASSISTANT", "content": "The frame looks like a fresh run (timer full at 64, blue at rows 16-19, cols 12-31). Executing the fast plan: align blue under magenta (rows 12-15, cols 20-39) and press SPACE.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\nr = action(['UP','RIGHT','RIGHT','SPACE'])\ndef bb(fr):\n    out={}\n    for n in fr.segmentation['nodes']:\n        if n['color'] in ('b','M','c','Y','N'):\n            b=n['boundary']; rs=[p[0] for p in b]; cs=[p[1] for p in b]\n            out[n['color']]= (min(rs),min(cs),max(rs),max(cs))\n    return out\nfor t in transitions[-4:]:\n    print(t.action, bb(t.before_frame), '->', bb(t.after_frame))\nprint({k: last_action_result.get(k) for k in ('board_changed','done','level_completed','game_over','run_complete')})\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL RESULT: python", "content": "UP {'N': (0, 0, 0, 63), 'c': (1, 36, 3, 39), 'M': (4, 36, 7, 39), 'b': (16, 12, 19, 31), 'Y': (52, 40, 59, 51)} -> {'N': (0, 0, 0, 61), 'c': (1, 36, 3, 39), 'M': (4, 36, 7, 39), 'b': (12, 12, 15, 